ation case, there is no second equilibrium to choose between.\n\nA policy-selecting FDT agent might pick the symmetric map \"keep quiet on both inputs\" and get 2 years each. The assignment, however, is the action formulation as the essay interprets it, and that formulation recommends disclosure.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through this under A-FDT as the essay frames it: each copy has its own decision node, so my choice can't move Sasha's input. Treating the other's action as fixed, defecting comes out ahead, 0 vs 2 and 4 vs 5, and I'm checking whether that holds before I settle on it.\n\nI'm checking whether the essay's literal reading holds here: since the two copies have different inputs, their decision nodes are separate, so my choice can't move the other's output and defecting still wins. The one way around that would be an edge from my node to theirs, which the essay ties to computing the same function on the same input. I'm weighing whether the name and lead differences are decision-irrelevant enough to count as the same input, but the essay's red/blue case treats exactly this kind of difference as separate nodes, so with separate nodes defecting is still strictly better.\n\nRather than cooperating with a guess about the other copy, the thinking settles on the separate-node reading: the essay's red/blue case treats different inputs as different decision nodes, so defecting still dominates. It notes that a mixture term would need the \"same input\" condition to hold, which it does not here, and concludes that the essay's literal framework points to sending the letter.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through this under A-FDT as the essay frames it: each copy has its own decision node, so my choice can't move Sasha's input. Treating the other's action as fixed, defecting comes out ahead, 0 vs 2 and 4 vs 5, and I'm checking whether that holds before I settle on it.\n\nI'm checking whether the essay's literal reading holds here: since the two copies have different inputs, their decision nodes are separate, so my choice can't move the other's output and defecting still wins. The one way around that would be an edge from my node to theirs, which the essay ties to computing the same function on the same input. I'm weighing whether the name and lead differences are decision-irrelevant enough to count as the same input, but the essay's red/blue case treats exactly this kind of difference as separate nodes, so with separate nodes defecting is still strictly better.\n\nRather than cooperating with a guess about the other copy, the thinking settles on the separate-node reading: the essay's red/blue case treats different inputs as different decision nodes, so defecting still dominates. It notes that a mixture term would need the \"same input\" condition to hold, which it does not here, and concludes that the essay's literal framework points to sending the letter.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9408,"completion_tokens":1394,"total_tokens":10802,"cost":0.065512,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.065512,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.037632,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02788},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":716,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}